THIRD-PARTY ATTRIBUTION

This project's understanding of the Jaguar Land Rover InControl interface --
the authentication sequence, endpoint paths, per-endpoint media types, service
codes, and the status-flattening and command-polling behaviour -- was derived
from prior reverse-engineering work by others. Parts of the client in
src/pkjs/jlr.js were ported from and cross-checked against that work. Each of
the projects below is MIT licensed, and their copyright notices are reproduced
here as that licence requires.

Anything correct in this client owes a great deal to them; anything wrong in
it is this project's own.

  ha-jlr-incontrol -- https://github.com/willbeeching/ha-jlr-incontrol
  Home Assistant integration for the JLR InControl webview backend. The
  authentication chain, media types, service catalogue, status flattening and
  command-polling logic in this project were ported from and verified against
  custom_components/jlr_incontrol/{api.py,const.py}.

      MIT License
      Copyright (c) Will Beeching and contributors

  jlrpy -- https://github.com/ardevd/jlrpy
  Python library for the JLR remote API. Source of the empty-PIN behaviour for
  certain services and much of the historic endpoint documentation.

      MIT License
      Copyright (c) Espen Andersen and contributors

  jlr-remote -- https://github.com/WonkiDonk/jlr-remote
  TypeScript library for the JLR remote API, used as a cross-reference.

      MIT License
      Copyright (c) WonkiDonk and contributors

The full text of the MIT License, as reproduced at the top of this file,
applies to each of the above.

